  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 254 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nSunday night, the low pressure system from the short term forecast \nperiod will continue to lift northeastward away along the Eastern \nSeaboard, with trailing rain showers coming to an end across the \narea. Overnight into Monday, a mid level shortwave will move across \nthe Tennessee Valley region, which will help advance a decaying cold \nfrontal boundary into northwest Georgia. Forcing ahead of the front \nwill be limited as it weakens and becomes more diffuse, with chances \nof showers being less than 10% as a result. Low temperatures on \nMonday morning will range from the upper 40s in the far northeastern \nmountains to the mid 50s in central Georgia. These lows will be from \n10 degrees above daily normals in east-central Georgia to as much as \n18 degrees above normal in far north Georgia. Highs on Monday \nafternoon will rise into the upper 60s in north Georgia and 70s \nelsewhere across the area. The progression of this front will begin \nto slow late in the day on Monday, with the remnant boundary \neventually stalling across central Georgia on Monday night. An open \nwave developing near the south Texas Gulf coast will move across the \ncentral Gulf coast on Monday night and across south Georgia on \nTuesday. Though the wave is trending south in the latest guidance, \nisolated showers remain possible in central Georgia of the area \nduring the daytime on Tuesday as this disturbance overruns the \nremnant boundary.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday morning, a strong (1040 mb) surface \nhigh is progged to move east of the Great Lakes, with a potent CAD \nwedge developing along the lee side of the Appalachians and into \nGeorgia. Low temperatures on Wednesday morning will range from the \nupper 30s in the far northeastern mountains to the mid 40s in \ncentral Georgia. Mid-range models meanwhile indicate a deep trough \ncrossing the Rockies into the Great Plains. Uncertainty remains with \nhow this trough will evolve and potentially interact with another \nshortwave over the western Gulf Coast. This will determine the \nspecifics of the weather conditions in the middle and later parts of \nnext week. At this time, the latest GFS indicates a closed low \ndeveloping near the Mississippi River Valley while latest ECMWF \ndevelops a closed low near the Great Lakes with open troughing \nextending to the south. In any case, this flow regime will result in \nsouthwesterly mid and upper level flow and deep moisture advection \nover Georgia. The combination of the wedge and this moist flow will \nlikely lead to a raw day on Wednesday, with cold rain and \ntemperatures struggling to get above 50.",
